      "writtenOn": "2021-04-26T22:59:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "As someone that only had cursory glances at what the bug was about, I still feel pretty out of the loop even after reading this CL. Maybe a bigger CL description here would help.\n\nFrom what I can tell: you have an overvoltage problem, so instead of letting the ln9310 do its own startup we override a few of its \"procedures\" by using those 4 registers (though we used those 4 registers for something else before, so now we have to live with a small loss of functionality?).\n\nAll of that should be in this commit message, but in better words than mine.",
      "revId": "789389c3230653ce59159a46edb72e515de36a31",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0b4e5866_2a28e13c",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 11,
      "author": {
        "id": 1113904
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-04-26T22:59:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We\u0027re going to need a lot more confidence that \"\" in regards of how to test and reproduce this.\n\nIf I have 2 boards, one with before and one firmware with after your CL I should be able to tell them apart just using TEST\u003d line.",
      "revId": "789389c3230653ce59159a46edb72e515de36a31",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8bf1a90f_68f9a492",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1451837
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-04-24T01:35:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "All,\n\nThis is the new software workaround to address https://partnerissuetracker.corp.google.com/issues/185308433.  I added some comments comparing the old workaround to this new one.\n\nI need some help from Google checking (and probably modifying) the code I implemented for the timeout.  The goal is to avoid sending the I2C command that actually writes register `LN9310_STARTUP_STANDBY_EN` to 0 if it has been longer than 100 ms since the previous I2C command (writing register `LN9310_LION_CTRL_MASK` to the value `LN9310_LION_CTRL_TEST_OFF_AND_UNLOCK`).  The way I have it written now is assuming `field_update8` can\u0027t be interrupted but I think that probably isn\u0027t true.  ",
      "revId": "789389c3230653ce59159a46edb72e515de36a31",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
